WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:04.550
Suppose a linear model receives
the raw hour number eight.

2
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:08.630
Its hourly contribution
is one weight times eight.

3
00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:12.610
Moving from eight to nine
always adds that same weight.

4
00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:16.710
One straight slope cannot
express a morning rise,

5
00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:19.370
a midday dip and an evening rise.

6
00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:21.690
There is also a clock boundary:

7
00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:26.910
twenty three and zero are adjacent
in time, yet far apart numerically.

8
00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:31.680
We need to choose a representation based
on what relationships the model should be

9
00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:32.770
able to learn.

10
00:00:34.692 --> 00:00:40.222
The first representation allocates twenty
four hour indicators, in hour order.

11
00:00:40.652 --> 00:00:44.062
At eight o'clock,
the hour eight indicator is one.

12
00:00:44.352 --> 00:00:46.222
The other twenty three are zero.

13
00:00:46.952 --> 00:00:51.102
A future linear model can assign a
separate coefficient to each hour.

14
00:00:51.532 --> 00:00:55.932
This adds flexibility without inventing
an ordinal distance between the category

15
00:00:55.932 --> 00:00:56.622
codes.

16
00:00:57.712 --> 00:00:59.342
Encoding creates a representation.

17
00:00:59.812 --> 00:01:04.092
It does not fit any of those coefficients
or guarantee that the resulting predictor

18
00:01:04.092 --> 00:01:05.262
will be accurate.

19
00:01:07.358 --> 00:01:12.788
We repeat that idea for seven weekday
categories and twelve month categories.

20
00:01:13.438 --> 00:01:18.608
Then we append the holiday and working
day flags, already zero or one.

21
00:01:19.058 --> 00:01:24.188
The total is twenty four plus
seven plus twelve plus two,

22
00:01:24.458 --> 00:01:25.968
which is forty five columns.

23
00:01:26.538 --> 00:01:29.048
The order is hour, weekday,

24
00:01:29.598 --> 00:01:29.928
month,

25
00:01:30.298 --> 00:01:30.848
holiday,

26
00:01:31.278 --> 00:01:31.908
working day.

27
00:01:32.578 --> 00:01:35.528
There is one shared
schema for all partitions.

28
00:01:36.318 --> 00:01:41.528
We save its column names so a later weight
vector always refers to the same meanings.

29
00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:47.700
The hour columns occupy positions
zero through twenty three.

30
00:01:48.110 --> 00:01:50.400
Hour eight activates position eight.

31
00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:52.540
Weekdays begin at twenty four,

32
00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:56.480
so Monday, code one,
activates position twenty five.

33
00:01:57.190 --> 00:01:58.500
Months begin at thirty one,

34
00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:01.280
so January activates position thirty one.

35
00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:04.940
Holiday at position
forty three stays zero.

36
00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:08.080
Working day at position forty four is one.

37
00:02:08.510 --> 00:02:11.820
The result has four ones
and forty one zeros.

38
00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:16.170
The recorded target of one hundred fifty
four stays outside these forty five

39
00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:17.040
values.

40
00:02:19.025 --> 00:02:22.795
Read the encoder's inner expression
as a membership question.

41
00:02:23.345 --> 00:02:26.135
Does this row's field equal this category?

42
00:02:26.645 --> 00:02:31.735
A match becomes floating point one,
and any other category becomes zero.

43
00:02:32.605 --> 00:02:36.355
The nested loop walks the category
groups in the declared order.

44
00:02:36.765 --> 00:02:40.735
Afterward, the two flags are
appended in their declared order.

45
00:02:41.365 --> 00:02:45.735
The code is short because the important
decisions are explicit in the schema:

46
00:02:46.645 --> 00:02:50.035
permitted fields,
category domains and column order.

47
00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:56.710
In a later additive linear model,
the bias contributes to every prediction.

48
00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:00.710
For this row,
the active hour eight, Monday

49
00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:03.570
and January coefficients also contribute.

50
00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:08.090
The working day coefficient
contributes because that flag is one.

51
00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:11.330
The holiday coefficient
is multiplied by zero.

52
00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:15.570
These terms describe the calculation
that a fitted model would make.

53
00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:19.390
There are no fitted feature weights
in this data preparation step.

54
00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:23.150
One hot encoding permits
separate category effects;

55
00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:27.870
this first additive form does not
create hour by weekday interactions.

56
00:03:29.942 --> 00:03:34.592
Now change only the encoder's
hour input from eight to nine.

57
00:03:35.162 --> 00:03:38.512
Hour eight switches off
and hour nine switches on.

58
00:03:39.002 --> 00:03:40.552
Monday, January

59
00:03:40.922 --> 00:03:42.612
and both flags stay unchanged.

60
00:03:43.462 --> 00:03:45.072
The feature count remains forty five.

61
00:03:46.062 --> 00:03:47.272
With fitted coefficients,

62
00:03:47.762 --> 00:03:52.082
the prediction difference would be the
hour nine weight minus the hour eight

63
00:03:52.082 --> 00:03:52.632
weight.

64
00:03:52.902 --> 00:03:55.572
This is a controlled encoding probe.

65
00:03:55.862 --> 00:03:59.602
It does not claim that the next real
observation has the same target,

66
00:03:59.602 --> 00:04:03.112
or that demand must rise
when the clock advances.

67
00:04:05.227 --> 00:04:08.377
One hot encoding treats
hour categories separately.

68
00:04:09.007 --> 00:04:12.917
It gives no built in preference for
neighbouring hours to behave alike.

69
00:04:13.447 --> 00:04:18.507
A sine and cosine pair can represent
position around the daily clock with two

70
00:04:18.507 --> 00:04:19.117
columns.

71
00:04:19.707 --> 00:04:23.537
Twenty three and zero then sit
close together on a circle.

72
00:04:24.087 --> 00:04:25.227
For a linear model,

73
00:04:25.227 --> 00:04:30.607
a single pair also constrains the daily
contribution to one smooth sinusoidal

74
00:04:30.607 --> 00:04:31.257
shape.

75
00:04:31.907 --> 00:04:35.337
We keep one hot encoding as
an inspectable first choice.

76
00:04:35.827 --> 00:04:40.537
A different encoding needs a controlled
comparison on the same validation hours.

77
00:04:42.483 --> 00:04:46.353
Let us follow the changed hour
through exact coordinates.

78
00:04:47.363 --> 00:04:51.213
The Monday eight o’clock input
activates positions eight,

79
00:04:51.723 --> 00:04:52.043
twenty five,

80
00:04:52.603 --> 00:04:53.073
thirty one

81
00:04:53.543 --> 00:04:54.073
and forty four.

82
00:04:54.883 --> 00:05:00.033
At nine o’clock, position eight
turns off and position nine turns on.

83
00:05:00.623 --> 00:05:03.093
The other active positions
remain unchanged.

84
00:05:04.123 --> 00:05:07.933
Both vectors still have forty
five values and four ones.

85
00:05:09.083 --> 00:05:13.193
Two coordinates changed,
even though only one source field changed.

86
00:05:13.623 --> 00:05:17.633
This is the concrete effect of
replacing one categorical choice.

87
00:05:19.775 --> 00:05:23.225
To connect this representation
to a linear prediction,

88
00:05:24.055 --> 00:05:25.305
choose illustrative coefficients.

89
00:05:26.355 --> 00:05:27.745
The intercept is ten,

90
00:05:28.295 --> 00:05:31.925
the hour eight coefficient twenty,
and the shared Monday,

91
00:05:32.275 --> 00:05:36.825
January and working day contributions
are three, two and five.

92
00:05:37.515 --> 00:05:38.485
That sums to forty.

93
00:05:39.355 --> 00:05:44.165
Give hour nine coefficient twelve and
the changed request sums to thirty two.

94
00:05:44.975 --> 00:05:48.955
The eight rental difference comes
entirely from selecting a different hour

95
00:05:48.955 --> 00:05:49.585
coefficient.

96
00:05:50.435 --> 00:05:53.025
These are hand chosen explanatory weights.

97
00:05:53.295 --> 00:05:57.645
They do not claim a trained prediction
or accuracy for source record fifty four.

98
00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:04.760
Now suppose somebody exchanges the
meanings of columns eight and nine but

99
00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:06.290
keeps the array length.

100
00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:10.630
Would checking forty five columns
and four ones detect the problem?

101
00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:12.550
Pause before answering.

102
00:06:13.580 --> 00:06:15.890
Both checks can still pass.

103
00:06:16.420 --> 00:06:20.530
A weight vector would now refer to
different hours, changing the calculation.

104
00:06:21.340 --> 00:06:25.800
The fixed feature names and saved schema
order are therefore part of the model

105
00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:26.570
contract.

106
00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:30.970
Shape is necessary, but it is not
the complete meaning of an input.
